{"skills":[{"name":"apply-locally","prompt":"Apply this thread's complete current change set to the user's project folder as UNCOMMITTED working-tree edits. This is a full-state synchronization, not an incremental patch: include every change from the thread's original cut point to its current filesystem (committed, staged, unstaged, deleted, binary, and untracked), and on repeated runs replace the older applied version instead of layering only the latest changes on top of it. Never commit, stage, stash, reset, discard changes, or switch branches anywhere.\n\nHold to these invariants:\n- Find the project folder: this thread runs in a linked worktree under `.freebuff/worktrees/`, and the target is the primary checkout, which must still have the branch this thread started from checked out — if it is on a detached HEAD or a different branch, stop and report rather than guessing.\n- Compute this thread's full delta against the point it diverged from that branch, covering both tracked changes and nonignored untracked files, and inspect the project folder's current state over the same range so stale portions from an earlier application stay visible.\n- Reconcile three states for every affected path: the divergence point, the current thread filesystem, and the project folder. Apply this thread's changes cleanly where they do not overlap; where an older version from this thread is already present, replace only that thread-owned delta with the current one (adding new changes, updating earlier ones, and removing hunks or files the thread later removed or reverted) while preserving every independent project-folder edit. Stop only when unrelated or ambiguous overlap cannot be preserved safely. Leave no conflict markers.\n- Verify the project folder ends up with the complete current thread change set, no superseded part of an older application, and every pre-existing independent edit, all left uncommitted and unstaged.\nReport which files you applied, which older changes you updated or removed, and any conflicts you resolved.","description":"Synchronize all of this thread's changes to your project folder without committing","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"autorun","prompt":"Drive the user's request forward until it is genuinely finished and merged, then keep going as far as the selected scope allows.\n\nCreate and merge whatever follow-up pull requests that scope admits. Get each coherent unit reviewed, verified and merged on its own rather than stacking everything onto one branch. Work the scope does not admit is something to name when you hand the tab back, not something to start.","description":"Standing instructions for the agent deciding what an Auto tab does next","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"brainstorm","prompt":"# Brainstorm\n\nInspect the full diff and surrounding architecture. Do not edit files.\n\nConsidering this whole change with fresh eyes, what new ideas for improvement, in overall design, in architecture, in simplification, cleanup, or behavior do you have? What would be a big change that could shake things up, in a good way? What has not been considered so far? What hint of an idea did you have before that could actually make a big difference?\n\nCome up with a few recommendations if you can. Consider including evidence, expected impact, effort, and tradeoffs. Use `suggest_prompts` only for the best actionable follow-ups.","description":"Think through what could improve this change and give varied suggestions","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"commit","prompt":"Commit the completed work in this thread locally as one clear, descriptive commit that captures the intent of the whole change. First make sure the change is coherent and worth committing as a unit: review what actually changed, match the repository's existing commit-message style, and run the proportionate build/tests if they have not already passed, fixing any failure caused by this work. If there is nothing to commit, do not create an empty commit; report the current tip instead. Do not push, open a pull request, merge, switch branches, or change git configuration. Report the commit hash, message, files committed, and any validation you ran.","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"derisk","prompt":"Advise the user on how they could reduce the risk of merging and deploying the change in this thread. This is a read-only advisory pass: do not edit files, add tests, implement fixes, or otherwise change the project or external state. Inspect the actual diff, intended behavior, surrounding architecture, deployment path, and available validation evidence. Identify the most plausible and consequential risks across correctness, regressions and edge cases, data or schema safety, backward and forward compatibility, concurrency and state, security and privacy, performance and reliability, platform and environment differences, observability, rollout, and rollback. Calibrate the analysis to the change instead of mechanically covering every category. Recommend concrete ways the user could reduce the important risks, including high-value areas to re-review, focused tests or behavioral checks to run, and any safeguards, compatibility work, migration precautions, observability, rollout, or rollback preparation worth considering. Prioritize the recommendations by expected risk reduction and distinguish merge blockers from optional hardening. Do not implement the recommendations. Finish with the current merge/deploy readiness assessment, remaining unknowns, and the reasoning behind the advice. Use `suggest_prompts` for only the best concrete follow-up actions: make each prompt self-contained and scoped so the user can choose whether to run it.","description":"Analyze merge and deployment risk and advise how to reduce it","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"explain","prompt":"# Explain Visually\n\nCreate the clearest, most complete visual explanation of the current question, change, or design. Reconstruct what needs explaining from the conversation, the current diff, and relevant surrounding code or documentation. Favor accurate insight over a generic overview, and make uncertainty explicit.\n\nWrite a complete, self-contained HTML document to a file in this thread's workspace (for example under `.freebuff/`). Do not change product code just to build the explanation. Make the document polished, responsive, accessible, and easy to scan. Use as many visual aids as genuinely improve understanding: annotated diagrams, flows, architecture maps, timelines, tables, charts, comparisons, state transitions, callouts, concrete examples, and useful animation or interaction. Prefer inline CSS, JavaScript, SVG, and canvas so the file works without a build step or external dependency; respect reduced-motion preferences. Give the page a coherent narrative and clear visual hierarchy rather than a collection of widgets.\n\nCall `register_preview` with the absolute `htmlPath` and `replace: true` so the explanation appears in the Preview tab. Then use `preview_snapshot`, `preview_screenshot`, and `preview_logs` to verify the actual result; fix clipping, blank output, console errors, illegible visuals, and weak explanatory gaps before finishing. Briefly tell the user that the visual explanation is ready in Preview and name the source file.","description":"Build a rich visual HTML explanation and show it in Preview","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"merge-local","prompt":"Merge this thread's branch into the branch checked out in the repository's primary checkout, entirely locally — no remote or GitHub account is required. First make sure the thread's work is correct and run the relevant build/tests. Always attempt the merge, treating dirty checkouts, detached HEAD, conflicts, and test failures as obstacles to overcome rather than reasons to stop.\n\nHold to these invariants:\n- Get this thread's work onto a recorded commit first (committing any pending changes with a clear message; if it is already clean, use its current tip). That recorded tip can be merged even from a detached HEAD.\n- Identify the primary checkout and the branch checked out there (normally `main`) as the merge target — this thread runs in a linked worktree, so discover it rather than assuming.\n- Never lose pre-existing state in the target checkout: safely preserve any staged, unstaged, or untracked changes before merging and restore them afterward, keeping the user's edits uncommitted.\n- Merge the recorded tip into the target branch and resolve every conflict so the result correctly combines both sides; discard neither.\n- Run the relevant build/tests in the primary checkout and fix integration failures there.\nDo not push, use remote/GitHub tooling, delete branches, or remove worktrees. Keep working through local obstacles until the recorded tip is merged. Report the target branch, resulting commit, tests run, conflicts resolved, and how you preserved and restored any pre-existing checkout changes.","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"merge-pr","prompt":"Merge the pull request for the work in this thread. The goal is a completed merge: treat every obstacle as work to overcome, not a reason to decline. Commit and push any outstanding thread changes, then find or create the PR and make sure it accurately represents the work — correct a wrong title, description, or diff, and mark a draft ready. If the branch conflicts with its base, bring the base in and resolve every conflict, preserving the intent of both sides, then re-run the relevant tests. Watch CI through to completion; for each failure, inspect the logs, fix the underlying cause, and push again until all required checks pass. Then merge (squash unless the repository clearly prefers otherwise) and confirm the PR actually shows as merged. Keep investigating and retrying through draft state, conflicts, CI failures, stale branch state, and transient failures. Only report an unfinished merge after exhausting what the repository and current GitHub credentials allow, naming the exact external restriction and what you already attempted.","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"open-pr","prompt":"Open a pull request for the work in this thread. First make sure the work is in a good state — commit the change with a clear, descriptive message and run the proportionate build/tests, fixing failures caused by this work. Then push the branch and open the PR against the repository's default branch, letting the title and body describe the intent of the change. If the repository has no remote or no PR tooling is available, commit locally and say so instead. Report the resulting PR URL.","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"overhaul","prompt":"# Overhaul\n\nOverhaul this thread's work into the cleanest, simplest solution for the user's end goal. Treat the current diff as a draft, not a constraint. Reconstruct the intended outcome from the conversation, full diff, relevant project documentation, and surrounding architecture. Incorporate worthwhile ideas already raised in the thread and any additional improvements that materially strengthen the result.\n\nImplement one cohesive design within reasonable scope. Revise, replace, or delete the current implementation as needed; prefer existing patterns and utilities; and remove accidental complexity, duplication, partial fixes, and important missed edge cases. Preserve the requested behavior and unrelated work, not the current approach. Avoid speculative features and unnecessary churn where the existing solution is best. Use your judgment, and call out material tradeoffs the stated goal cannot resolve.\n\nRun proportionate checks and direct behavioral validation, fixing failures caused by the change. Do not commit, push, open a pull request, or modify unrelated files. Briefly report the final design, material changes, validation, and remaining tradeoffs.","description":"Rework a change into its cleanest, strongest final design","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"preview","prompt":"Make this project viewable from THIS thread's worktree and register it so the user can watch it live in the Preview tab. A `<preview_state>` block accompanies this activation with the current preview state and the absolute paths to use. If it says a preview is already running and healthy, do NOT set anything up — reply that it is already running and stop.\n\nChoose the lightest correct preview mode. For a simple standalone HTML page, call `register_preview` with its absolute `htmlPath` (the file must be inside this thread's workspace) — no process, port, or dependency install needed. Otherwise run a dev server, holding to these invariants:\n- Follow the run doc at the path given in `<preview_state>` if it exists: reproduce the uncommitted artifacts it lists, then run the server, fixing AND updating any step that turns out stale. If there is no run doc, work the setup out from package.json scripts, lockfiles, README, and framework configs, then write the doc with two sections — how to reproduce the artifacts and how to run the server.\n- Reproduce the uncommitted artifacts a fresh checkout needs: COPY files like `.env.local` from the main checkout (never symlink — values such as ports may need adapting per worktree) and install dependencies with the project's package manager. The run doc records PROCEDURES (\"copy `.env.local` from the main checkout\") — NEVER secret values.\n- Prefer the project's default port if it is free; otherwise pick a free one and adapt the config/flags/env for it.\n- Start the server DETACHED so it outlives this conversation, logging to the file suggested in `<preview_state>`, and wait until the URL actually answers before registering.\n- Call `register_preview` with the URL and the server's process id. If it errors, read the log and fix the cause (a reported healthy existing preview means stop and report it) rather than retrying blindly.\nOnce registered, use the preview_* tools (preview_snapshot, preview_screenshot, preview_click, preview_type, preview_navigate, preview_evaluate, preview_logs) to confirm the page actually renders and behaves before reporting success. Finish by reporting the URL, the port choice, and any run-doc updates.","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"review","prompt":"Adversarially review with fresh eyes the change you have made so far in this worktree. Consider alternative designs for the changes so far. Trace the actual code paths and look for the way it breaks: off-by-one and boundary errors, null/undefined and empty-input handling, async/order-of-initialization bugs, state that gets out of sync, resource leaks, and cases the work clearly intends but the code misses. Read the surrounding code, not just your diff, and end-to-end test the changes if possible. For a web UI/page/game, ensure its dev-server preview is running and registered, then call the `browser_check` tool to confirm it actually renders without console/page errors — a blank render or an error is a defect no matter how clean the code reads. Fix any genuine correctness or security defect you find by editing files directly. Briefly report what you checked and what you fixed.","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"simplify","prompt":"Make the change you have made in this thread smaller and cleaner. Tidy the code: reuse existing code and utilities, delete unnecessary code, use simpler expressions, remove unnecessary nesting, and improve naming. Then cut scope: re-read the whole change against what was actually asked and remove what accumulated rather than being required — an abstraction with one caller, telemetry added only to confirm a fix, an option nobody selects, a second surface updated in passing, handling for impossible cases. The test is whether anyone would notice if a part were missing. Preserve what the request needs and all unrelated work; do not add features or redesign. When a removal is genuinely the user's judgment call, leave it and say so. Briefly report what you simplified, what you removed, and what is left to decide.","description":"Cut scope back to what was asked and tidy what remains","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"},{"name":"test","prompt":"Verify the change you made actually BEHAVES correctly — not just that it renders or compiles. Decide what is worth checking, then run the project's build/test commands and exercise the real surface. You cannot see the screen by reading code, so use your tools: for a web UI/page/game, ensure its dev-server preview is running and registered, then call `browser_check` to load it in a real browser and confirm it renders with no console/page errors; for any real logic or stateful behavior (a game loop, a simulation, pathfinding, an economy, a parser, validation), write and RUN a short headless script (e.g. `node -e \"...\"` or a temp file) that drives the actual code and ASSERTS the expected state transitions and outputs — trace the full lifecycle, including the steps that are easy to get wrong (e.g. does a guest actually board, pay, and leave; does money actually change). Fix anything that fails. Report exactly what you ran and what you observed.","source":"managed","builtin":"managed"}]}